---
import Base from '../layouts/base.astro';
import { brand, site } from '../lib/site';
import { publishedPosts, chain, utcDay } from '../lib/trust';

const posts = await publishedPosts('en');
const ch = await chain();
const recent = posts.slice(0, 10).map((p) => ({
  date: utcDay(p.data.date),
  title: p.data.title,
  zone: p.collection,
  hash: ch.byId.get(p.id) ?? '',
}));
---

<Base
  title="How to verify this record"
  description="Why this site is worth trusting: hash chain, deployment time, and external anchors — plus the limits."
  path="/verify"
  lang="en"
  switchHref="/zh/verify">
  <section class="wrap measure flow">
    <h1>How to verify this record</h1>

    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Publishing "what I have done" is cheap. What is expensive is letting a reader confirm that the same person really
        did it, in that order, over that time. Three mechanisms do that here.
      </p>

      <h2>1. Hash chain: the head tells you if the body changed</h2>
      <p>
        At build time every published entry is sorted by date and chained: each digest =
        SHA256(previous digest | index | file id | ISO8601 date | title | <strong>full body</strong>).
        Change a title, a date, or one character of prose, and every digest after it — including the head — changes.
        The summary field used in listings is not hashed.
      </p>
      <p>
        Stated limits: this proves <em>nothing was edited after publication</em>. It cannot prove <em>the writing is true</em>.
        The head is stamped by a Cloudflare Pages deployment; the source sits in Git (the footer names the commit this
        content was built from). Together they form checkable time evidence.
      </p>
      <div class="card stack-sm">
        <p class="meta">Current head ({ch.count} entries, built by this deployment)</p>
        <p class="mono" style="word-break: break-all">{ch.head}</p>
        <p class="meta">
          Machine-readable chain: <a href="/chain.json" class="mono">/chain.json</a>
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2>2. Deployment time: the content is stamped by the platform</h2>
      <p>
        The site is a static build; deployment output cannot be rewritten server-side. What you see matches the files a
        given deployment uploaded, and the page footer names the commit that produced it.
      </p>

      <h2>3. External anchors: one person, several places</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>GitHub: <a href={`https://github.com/${site.anchors.github}`} rel="me">github.com/{site.anchors.github}</a> — public commit history is the hardest engineering evidence.</li>
        <li>
          Source of this site:
          <a href={`https://github.com/${site.anchors.github}/personal-site`} rel="me">github.com/{site.anchors.github}/personal-site</a>
          — the footer names the commit this page was built from. Check that commit out, run 'pnpm build', and the chain
          head in the footer should match: a reproducible anchor, not a claim.
        </li>
        <li>Email: <a href={`mailto:${site.anchors.email}`} rel="me">{site.anchors.email}</a> — the domain is mine and receives mail.</li>
        <li>RSS: <a href="/rss.xml">/rss.xml</a> (English feed) — aggregators can archive it externally.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Not done yet (stated plainly)</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Periodically anchoring the head somewhere immutable (a public repo, an RFC3161 timestamp authority).</li>
        <li>Signing the head with a private key and publishing the key fingerprint.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Digests of the ten most recent entries</h2>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Date (UTC)</th><th>Section</th><th>Title</th><th>Digest</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {
            recent.map((r) => (
              <tr>
                <td>{r.date}</td>
                <td>{r.zone}</td>
                <td>{r.title}</td>
                <td class="mono">{r.hash.slice(0, 12)}</td>
              </tr>
            ))
          }
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
